I need to interpret the problem carefully. As literally written, $f=(1+|x|^\alpha)/(1+|\log|x||^\beta)$ is **never** in $L^p(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (the numerator either grows polynomially or stays bounded while the denominator grows at most logarithmically, so the tail at infinity always diverges). Since the problem asks for "conditions on $\alpha$ **and** $\beta$" — implying both parameters matter — the meaningful reading is the standard exercise with the factors swapped:

$$f(x)=\frac{1+|\log|x||^\beta}{1+|x|^\alpha}.$$

I solve this (intended) form.

---

**Theorem.** For $1\le p<\infty$, $n\ge 1$, 
$$f\in L^p(\mathbb{R}^n)\iff \alpha p>n\ \text{and}\ \beta p>-1.$$

**Proof.** Since $f$ is radial, set $r=|x|$. By polar coordinates,
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} f(x)^p\,dx=|S^{n-1}|\int_0^\infty \left(\frac{1+|\log r|^\beta}{1+r^\alpha}\right)^p r^{n-1}\,dr=:|S^{n-1}|\,I.$$
It suffices to determine when $I<\infty$. The integrand
$$h(r)=\left(\frac{1+|\log r|^\beta}{1+r^\alpha}\right)^p r^{n-1}$$
is continuous on $(0,\infty)\setminus\{1\}$. We examine three regions: $r\to\infty$, $r\to 0$, and $r\to 1$ (where $|\log r|=0$).

**1. Tail at infinity ($r\to\infty$).**
- If $\alpha\le 0$: $1+r^\alpha\le 2$ while $1+|\log r|^\beta\ge 1$, so $h(r)\ge 2^{-p}r^{n-1}$. Since $\int^\infty r^{n-1}dr=\infty$ ($n\ge 1$), $I$ diverges. Hence **$\alpha>0$ is necessary**.
- Assume $\alpha>0$. Then $1+r^\alpha\sim r^\alpha$, and $1+|\log r|^\beta\sim(\log r)^\beta$ if $\beta>0$, while it stays bounded between positive constants if $\beta\le 0$. Thus $h(r)\asymp (\log r)^{\beta p}\,r^{n-1-\alpha p}$ (with $(\log r)^{\beta p}$ bounded when $\beta\le 0$).
  - If $\alpha p>n$: pick $\varepsilon>0$ with $\varepsilon<\alpha p-n$. Then $n-1-\alpha p<-1-\varepsilon$, and $(\log r)^{\beta p}=o(r^{\varepsilon})$, so $h(r)\le C r^{-1-\varepsilon/2}$ for large $r$; the tail converges.
  - If $\alpha p<n$: $h(r)\ge c\,r^{n-1-\alpha p}$ with $n-1-\alpha p>-1$; the tail diverges.
  - If $\alpha p=n$: $h(r)\asymp(\log r)^{\beta p}r^{-1}$, and $\int_2^\infty(\log r)^{\beta p}r^{-1}dr=\int_{\log 2}^\infty u^{\beta p}\,du$ converges iff $\beta p<-1$.

So at infinity: $\alpha p>n$ works for **all** $\beta$; $\alpha p=n$ requires $\beta p<-1$; $\alpha p<n$ fails.

**2. Near the origin ($r\to 0$).** Assume $\alpha>0$ (already necessary). Then $1+r^\alpha\to 1$. As $r\to 0$, $|\log r|\to\infty$: if $\beta>0$, $1+|\log r|^\beta\sim|\log r|^\beta$; if $\beta\le 0$, it stays bounded (tends to $1$). Hence $h(r)\lesssim |\log r|^{\max(\beta p,0)}r^{n-1}$. Since $|\log r|^m=o(r^{-1/2})$ for every $m>0$, we get $h(r)\le C r^{n-3/2}$ near $0$, integrable because $n-3/2>-1$ ($n\ge 1$). **The origin imposes no restriction** (given $\alpha>0$).

**3. Near the unit sphere ($r\to 1$).** Here $|\log r|\sim|r-1|$. The factor $1+r^\alpha\to 2$ stays bounded and bounded away from $0$. The behavior of $1+|\log r|^\beta$ depends on the sign of $\beta$:
- $\beta\ge 0$: $|\log r|^\beta\to 0$, so $1+|\log r|^\beta\to 1$; $h$ is bounded near $r=1$. No restriction.
- $\beta<0$: $|\log r|^\beta\to+\infty$, so $1+|\log r|^\beta\sim|r-1|^\beta$, and $h(r)\asymp |r-1|^{\beta p}$. The integral $\int_{1-\delta}^{1+\delta}|r-1|^{\beta p}dr$ converges iff $\beta p>-1$.

So near $r=1$: the condition is **$\beta p>-1$** (automatic when $\beta\ge 0$, restrictive when $\beta<0$).

**4. Synthesis.** Combining the three regions:
- $\alpha p>n$ makes the tail converge (for every $\beta$); the origin is harmless; the sphere requires $\beta p>-1$.
- $\alpha p=n$ requires $\beta p<-1$ at infinity but $\beta p>-1$ at the sphere — **impossible**.
- $\alpha p<n$ diverges at infinity.

Therefore
$$f\in L^p(\mathbb{R}^n)\iff \alpha p>n\ \text{and}\ \beta p>-1,$$
i.e. $\alpha>\dfrac{n}{p}$ and $\beta>-\dfrac{1}{p}$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
